     1	"""Thin MCP server wrapper around the Research Foundry Search Router.
     2	
     3	**CARP-5.2 (catalog-assisted-research-planning) additions.** The core
     4	``search_run`` tool and every mode-preset tool below also accept three
     5	optional, keyword-style JSON arguments that marshal straight through to
     6	:func:`router.run_search`'s own keyword-only ``identity`` /
     7	``sensitivity_threshold`` / ``evidence_plan`` parameters (carp-contract-
     8	freeze.md §2, §4). They are **context**, not policy -- ``retrieval.policy``
     9	and ``retrieval.limits`` already ride inside ``request`` itself (validated
    10	against ``search_request.schema.yaml``) and need no wrapper change. All
    11	three default to ``None``, reproducing the pre-CARP call exactly:
    12	
    13	* ``identity`` -- a plain ``{"user_id": ..., "workspace_id": ..., "roles":
    14	  [...]}`` mapping (MCP arguments are JSON, never Python objects). Marshaled
    15	  into an :class:`AuthIdentity` immediately before the call; never inspected
    16	  or re-shaped here (no business logic -- see the module docstring's "thin
    17	  transport adapter" rule below).
    18	* ``sensitivity_threshold`` -- forwarded verbatim. Per
    19	  ``catalog_retrieval.RetrievalConstraints``, an *omitted* threshold is not
    20	  defaulted to "allow everything" -- it is threaded through as ``None``,
    21	  which the P2 adapter denies fail-closed. This wrapper never invents one.
    22	* ``evidence_plan`` -- an already-built ``research_evidence_plan`` dict (the
    23	  same shape :mod:`planning` persists). When supplied, ``run_search``
    24	  consumes it as-is instead of building an ad-hoc single-question plan.
    25	
    26	
    27	This module exposes the router's Python API (``run_search`` / ``extract_urls``)
    28	as a small set of MCP tools, matching the minimum surface from spec §10.2.
    29	
    30	**Tool naming convention.** Tool names are the underscored Python function
    31	names the ``@server.tool()`` decorator derives by default (e.g.
    32	``search_run``, ``search_source_discovery``) — *not* the dotted form
    33	(``search.run``) some early spec drafts used. This is the single source of
    34	truth for registered tool names; ``docs/dev/architecture/search-router/
    35	deployment.md`` §5 must be kept in sync with this list:
    36	
    37	* ``search_run``                  → :func:`router.run_search`
    38	* ``extract_url``                 → :func:`router.extract_urls`
    39	* ``search_source_discovery``     → ``run_search`` with ``mode="source_discovery"``
    40	* ``search_semantic_discovery``   → ``run_search`` with ``mode="semantic_discovery"``
    41	* ``search_github_discovery``     → ``run_search`` with ``mode="github_discovery"``
    42	* ``search_quick_lookup``         → ``run_search`` with ``mode="quick_lookup"``
    43	* ``search_official_sources``     → ``run_search`` with ``mode="official_source_check"``
    44	* ``search_academic_discovery``   → ``run_search`` with ``mode="academic_discovery"``
    45	
    46	**Offline-safe import contract.** The module itself MUST import successfully
    47	without the ``mcp`` SDK installed. Only :func:`build_server` (and therefore
    48	:func:`main`) attempt to import the SDK, and they raise a clear
    49	:class:`RuntimeError` telling the operator to ``uv sync --extra mcp`` if it
    50	isn't there. This mirrors the rest of the router's adapter pattern: optional
    51	deps are lazy, never top-level.
    52	
    53	Tools return the raw run dict / extraction dict produced by the router (which
    54	includes ``run_id``, ``source_cards``, ``schema_errors`` etc.). The MCP SDK
    55	serializes that to the client. No business logic lives here — this file is a
    56	thin transport adapter.
